Add: readable "KV EVICT" logging for VRAM and RAM tier reclaims,
completing the LOAD/STORE picture in v1/kv_offload/transfer_log.py
(patches/kv-transfer-request-logging.patch,
patches/kv-transfer-log-clarity.patch) with the third leg of the KV
lifecycle: when content actually leaves a tier, not just when it moves
between tiers.

Requested directly: watching KV LOAD/STORE lines answers "did this
request's context come off the disk" but not "did an earlier
conversation's context get reclaimed to make room" -- the user wants to
know when a conversation is (partially or fully) evicted from VRAM
(should already be backed up in RAM) or from RAM (should already be on
a secondary tier/disk if one is configured).

Two real reclaim points, both already existed and already had *some*
telemetry (a DEBUG log line at one, an opt-in KVCacheEvent/
OffloadingEvent sink at both) but nothing readable at INFO:

1. VRAM: v1/core/block_pool.py's `_maybe_evict_cached_block()`, called
   from `get_new_blocks()` (new allocation reclaiming a free-listed,
   previously-cached block) and `evict_blocks()` (explicit connector-
   driven invalidation). This is genuinely request-agnostic: prefix-cache
   blocks are content-addressed and can be shared by multiple
   conversations with the same prefix, so there is no single "owning
   request" to attribute a reclaim to in general -- reported as "-",
   matching transfer_log.py's own docstring, which already anticipated
   this ("Evictions may not have one; pass '-'").

2. RAM: v1/kv_offload/cpu/manager.py's `prepare_store()`, where
   `to_evict` (blocks the LRU/ARC eviction policy reclaims to make room
   for an incoming store) is computed. Same content-addressing caveat.
   By the time a RAM block is evictable here (ref_cnt == 0),
   TieringOffloadingManager.complete_store()'s cascade-protection has
   already released it -- meaning any configured secondary (fs) tier
   already has its own copy, matching the user's stated assumption
   ("at which point it should already be on disk").

Both reclaim points fire on essentially every reused block during normal
steady-state operation (not just under memory pressure) -- multiple
times per scheduling step under sustained load. Logging one line each
would reintroduce exactly the per-iteration noise problem this same
session already fixed elsewhere (ITERATION_LOG). Added a small batching
accumulator (`_EvictAccumulator` in transfer_log.py) instead: each
reclaim point calls `log_vram_evict()`/`log_ram_evict()` with the count
from ONE batch (one get_new_blocks()/evict_blocks()/prepare_store()
call, already naturally batched -- no new hot-path overhead), and the
accumulator only actually emits a "KV EVICT" line at most once per
VLLM_KV_EVICT_LOG_INTERVAL_S seconds (default 10, independent of
VLLM_LOG_STATS_INTERVAL), reporting the window's totals and its real
elapsed duration. Respects the existing VLLM_KV_TRANSFER_LOG=0 switch
(no separate on/off toggle -- EVICT is part of the same "watch a live
server" feature LOAD/STORE already are).

Token counts: VRAM eviction can use BlockPool's own `hash_block_size`
(the uniform block-size-in-tokens already used for prefix-cache hashing,
same value truncate_computed_blocks/scheduler.py's `self.block_size`
represent elsewhere in this codebase) for an exact blocks*block_size
figure. RAM eviction is blocks-only, no token estimate -- cpu/manager.py
works in abstract OffloadKey/block terms with no chunk-size knowledge at
that layer, matching the existing precedent already in this module (the
"fs -> RAM" staged-promotion leg reports blocks only for the same
reason).

Sample output:

    KV EVICT -                         | VRAM -> gone |  96 blk /  15360 tok (reclaimed for reuse, last 10.0s)
    KV EVICT -                         | RAM  -> gone |   6 blk (reclaimed for reuse, last 10.0s)

Verification: syntax-checked (ast.parse), `import
vllm.v1.core.block_pool` / `import vllm.v1.kv_offload.cpu.manager`
succeed standalone (no circular-import issue introduced by importing
transfer_log.py from either), a standalone accumulator smoke test
(override the interval, call add() below/above threshold, confirm no
premature flush and a correctly-formatted line with accurate elapsed
time once it crosses), `verify.sh --no-server` green. Not yet observed
against live traffic in this session.
