+An EVICT line, by contrast, IS a real removal -- content-addressed cache
+blocks reclaimed for reuse because the tier below them was (assumed)
+already full/protected while any pending backup was in flight (VRAM
+blocks are ref-count-protected until the offloading connector's own
+store has been submitted; RAM blocks are ref-count-protected while a
+secondary-tier cascade is in flight -- see
+patches/offload-flush-protect-running-request-blocks.patch and
+TieringOffloadingManager.complete_store()). EVICT has no single owning
+request -- content-addressed cache blocks can be shared by multiple
+conversations with the same prefix, so it's reported request-agnostic
+("-") and batched: individual block reclaims happen far too often to log
+one line each (every reused block under normal steady-state operation,
+not just under memory pressure), so they're accumulated and flushed at
+most once per VLLM_KV_EVICT_LOG_INTERVAL_S seconds (default 10) -- "last
+Ns" in the line is that window's actual elapsed time, not a fixed tick.
+
 A full disk-backed resume therefore reads top to bottom as
 ``fs -> RAM`` then ``RAM -> VRAM``, which is the staged promotion the tiering
 manager actually performs (secondary tiers cannot touch GPU memory directly).
 
